      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Measuring FE(NO) is a novel and non-invasive way to monitor airway inflammation (e.g. asthma). This clinical study was designed to investigate whether drinking ethanol might distort FE(NO) measurements."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Twenty healthy subjects drank 0.40 g ethanol/kg body weight in 15 min. Measurement of FE(NO) started approximately 30 min before drinking and at various times afterwards for 4 h post-dosing. Ethanol concentrations were determined in venous blood by gas chromatography and in end-exhaled breath by infra-red spectrometry."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The within subject standard deviation for determination of FE(NO) was 1.3 ppb, corresponding to a CV of 7.7%. The mean change in FE(NO) from pre-drinking levels during the 4h testing was statistically significant (P<0.001) according to repeated measures ANOVA. In absolute units the mean change was small, -2.01 and -1.94 ppb at 3 and 4h post-dosing, respectively (P<0.013, P<0.005)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "FE(NO) measurements were reproducible even in subjects with moderate concentrations of ethanol in blood and breath. The small decrease in FE(NO) observed at 3 and 4 h post-drinking was less than the intra-subject variations in FE(NO) measurements. The breath-alcohol concentrations in this study exceed all other endogenous volatiles, thus making it unlikely that other substances in human breath will bias the FE(NO) measurements."
        }
      ]},
